var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#Ginger.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Jinja-style template engine for Julia. Ginger compiles templates to Julia code during macro expansion, so rendering is ordinary, type-specialized Julia. There is no runtime parsing, no runtime compilation, and no module generated at runtime.\n\nThe syntax is inspired by OteraEngine.jl and Jinja2. The execution model is different, and Ginger is not a drop-in replacement for either. See Migrating from OteraEngine for the differences.","title":"Ginger.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Why-compile-at-macro-expansion-time?","page":"Home","text":"The host package reads each template once while it compiles. Ginger turns the template into ordinary Julia functions and splices those functions into the host module. The host package's precompile image caches the result, so there is no runtime loader, no cache, no Core.eval, and no world-age handling.\n\nAt render time, a template is a typed Julia function call. Context variables are NamedTuple fields, block dispatch is a NamedTuple lookup that constant-folds, and filters are plain function calls.","title":"Why compile at macro-expansion time?"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"module MyApp\n\nusing Ginger\nusing Ginger.DefaultHelpers     # upper, lower, excerpt, …\n\n@templates \"templates\" as TPL\n\nend\n\nGinger.render(TPL.index; user = \"frank\", posts = posts)   # -> String\nGinger.render!(stdout, TPL.index; user = \"frank\")\n\ntemplates/index.html:\n\nHello, {{ user }}!\n{% for post in posts %}\n  <article>{{ post.title }}</article>\n{% else %}\n  <p>No posts yet.</p>\n{% endfor %}\n\nrender builds an IOBuffer, calls the generated entry function, and returns a String. render! writes to any IO. A Template is also callable:\n\nTPL.index(; user = \"frank\")      # shorthand for render(TPL.index; user = \"frank\")\nTPL.index(io; user = \"frank\")    # shorthand for render!(io, TPL.index; user = \"frank\")","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Feature-summary","page":"Home","text":"One grammar. {% %} holds arbitrary Julia statements, and {{ }} holds an arbitrary Julia expression. Julia's parser matches nesting and precedence.\nInferred context. Free-variable analysis decides which names come from the render context. There is no {% context %} declaration.\nHTML escaping by default. escape is idempotent and safe bypasses it.\nComposition. {% macro %}, {% include %}, {% import %}, {% from %}, and static inheritance through {% extends %}, {% block %}, and super().\nHTML stack traces. Backtraces point at templates/index.html:42, and a render error reports the include/extends provenance chain.\nPrecompilation-aware. Editing a template invalidates the host package through Base.include_dependency, so the standard Julia dev loop re-expands it.","title":"Feature summary"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation-map","page":"Home","text":"Page Contents\nSyntax Delimiters, whitespace control, raw, comments, control flow\nContext Inferred context, undefined modes\nFilters and helpers Pipe filters, DefaultHelpers, custom helpers\nComposition and inheritance Macros, includes, imports, extends/block/super\nErrors Caret diagnostics, provenance chains, virtual paths\nTemplates and precompilation @template, @templates, the dev loop\nAPI reference The public API\nMigrating from OteraEngine Differences and a step-by-step migration","title":"Documentation map"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"This page lists the public API. Anything named __ginger_* is internal and may change without notice. The same applies to the fields and constructors of the types listed here unless a docstring says otherwise.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Macros","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Macros"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Rendering","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Rendering"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Configuration","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Escaping","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Escaping"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Errors","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Helpers","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Helpers"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#Ginger.@template","page":"API reference","text":"@template path [as NAME] [config = CONFIG]\n\nCompile the template at path during macro expansion and bind a Template value to NAME (default: the uppercased file stem) in the host module.\n\nThe file is read at expansion time. Base.include_dependency registers it, so editing the template invalidates the host package. path must be a string literal. A relative path is resolved against the directory of the file that contains the macro call.\n\n@template \"emails/welcome.html\" as WELCOME\nrender(WELCOME; user = \"frank\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.@template"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#Ginger.@templates","page":"API reference","text":"@templates path [as NAME] [config = CONFIG] [helpers = (Mod, …)]\n\nCompile every template under the directory path during macro expansion and bind a NamedTuple of Templates to NAME (default TEMPLATES) in the host module. Subdirectories become nested NamedTuples, and a file's key is its file stem, so views/partials/head.html is reached as TPL.partials.head.\n\nEvery file and every directory under path is read at expansion time and registered with Base.include_dependency, so editing a template, adding one, or removing one invalidates the host package and re-expands the macro. References ({% extends %}, {% include %}, {% import %}, {% from %}) are resolved inside the set, and a shared template is compiled once.\n\nhelpers = (MyHelpers, MyFilters) emits using MyHelpers, MyFilters into the host module before the templates are compiled, so helper functions and macros are available to every template. The entries are module names, not values.\n\n@templates \"templates\" as TPL\n@templates \"templates\" helpers = (MyHelpers,) config = Config(autoescape = false)\nrender(TPL.index; user = \"frank\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.@templates"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#Ginger.@ginger_str","page":"API reference","text":"ginger\"source\"\n\nCompile an inline template string during macro expansion and return a Template value. The string must be a literal. The template is compiled with the default Config; any reference ({% include %}, …) resolves relative to the file that contains the macro call.\n\nBecause the source is part of the host package's AST, an inline template is precompiled like a file-based one and needs no include_dependency entry.\n\nt = ginger\"Hello {{ name }}!\"\nt(name = \"frank\")          # -> \"Hello frank!\"\nrender(t; name = \"frank\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.@ginger_str"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Ginger.render","page":"API reference","text":"render(template; kwargs...) -> String\n\nRender template to a String. Keyword arguments make up the render context.\n\nIf rendering throws and the native backtrace contains at least one generated template function, the exception is wrapped in a TemplateError that carries the include/extends provenance chain. Otherwise the original exception propagates unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.render"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Ginger.render!","page":"API reference","text":"render!(io, template; kwargs...) -> io\n\nRender template to io. Errors are wrapped as for render.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.render!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Ginger.Template","page":"API reference","text":"Template\n\nA compiled template. path is the package-relative virtual path. entry is the generated entry function entry(out; kwargs...). sources is the compilation unit's provenance registry, mapping each generated function name to its SourceInfo, and source_root resolves virtual paths to absolute paths for diagnostics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.Template"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Ginger.Config","page":"API reference","text":"Config(; kwargs...)\n\nImmutable compilation configuration for a template unit. There is no TOML file and no mutable environment object: a Config is an ordinary value passed as a macro argument.\n\nKeywords\n\nexpression_start/expression_end (default \"{{\"/\"}}\"): expression delimiters.\nstatement_start/statement_end (default \"{%\"/\"%}\"): statement delimiters.\ncomment_start/comment_end (default \"{#\"/\"#}\"): comment delimiters.\ntrim_blocks (default false): remove the first newline after a block tag.\nlstrip_blocks (default false): remove whitespace from the start of a line to a block tag.\nautospace (default nothing): when true, enables trim_blocks and lstrip_blocks. When false, disables both. Overrides the individual flags.\nautoescape (default true): HTML-escape every {{ }} expression.\nsource_root (default pwd()): base directory used to resolve the virtual template paths stored in generated code.\nundefined (default :strict): missing-context-variable policy. One of :strict (throw), :lenient (bind Undefined), :default (bind Undefined and make the default(value, fallback) helper useful).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.Config"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Ginger.HTMLString","page":"API reference","text":"HTMLString(s)\n\nA string that is already safe for HTML output. escape is the identity on an HTMLString, which makes escaping idempotent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.HTMLString"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Ginger.escape","page":"API reference","text":"escape(x) -> HTMLString\n\nHTML-escape x for safe interpolation. Idempotent: escape(escape(x)) == escape(x), and escape(::HTMLString) is the identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.escape"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Ginger.safe","page":"API reference","text":"safe(x) -> HTMLString\n\nMark x as already HTML-safe, bypassing autoescaping. Idempotent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.safe"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Ginger.default","page":"API reference","text":"default(x, fallback)\n\nReturn fallback when x is an Undefined context variable, else x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.default"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Ginger.TemplateSyntaxError","page":"API reference","text":"TemplateSyntaxError(msg, pos=nothing; source=nothing)\n\nRaised at macro-expansion time when a template cannot be lexed, parsed, or normalized. pos points at the offending template position when one is known.\n\nsource is the full template text. When it is present, Base.showerror renders the offending line with a caret under pos. compile_template! attaches the source to every error that belongs to the template it is compiling, so macro users get carets without doing anything.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.TemplateSyntaxError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Ginger.TemplateError","page":"API reference","text":"TemplateError(cause, chain)\n\nWraps an exception raised while rendering a template, together with the provenance chain recovered from the native backtrace. cause is the original exception. render throws this only when at least one template frame is found. Otherwise the original exception propagates unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.TemplateError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Ginger.TemplateFrame","page":"API reference","text":"TemplateFrame\n\nOne entry in a TemplateError provenance chain. path is the package-relative virtual path (or the host source file for the final :render frame). abs_path is that path resolved against the template's Config.source_root. line and col locate the failing statement inside the template (col is 0 when the native backtrace does not carry a column).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.TemplateFrame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Ginger.MissingContextVariable","page":"API reference","text":"MissingContextVariable(name, path, line)\n\nRaised at render time when the :strict undefined mode is active and a context variable was not passed to render.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.MissingContextVariable"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Ginger.template_backtrace","page":"API reference","text":"template_backtrace(err::TemplateError) -> Vector{TemplateFrame}\ntemplate_backtrace() -> Vector{TemplateFrame}\n\nReturn the structured provenance chain of a TemplateError: one TemplateFrame per generated body, block, or macro that took part in the failing render, innermost first, followed by the host call site of render. Each frame carries the package-relative path and the same path resolved to an absolute abs_path under the template's Config.source_root.\n\nThe no-argument form returns the chain of the TemplateError currently being handled, or an empty vector when there is none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.template_backtrace"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#Ginger.DefaultHelpers","page":"API reference","text":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers\n\nA standard set of template helpers and filters. Import the module into the host module to make its names resolvable from templates:\n\nusing Ginger\nusing Ginger.DefaultHelpers\n\nThe scope pass treats every name imported into the host module as a global, so templates call these helpers with no registry and no explicit declaration.\n\nFilters follow the engine convention: a one-argument function is used directly ({{ name |> upper }}), and a filter that takes extra arguments returns a callable ({{ title |> excerpt(80) }}).\n\nescape, safe, HTMLString, and default are re-exported from Ginger. Generated code references escape fully qualified, so autoescaping works even without this import.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Ginger.DefaultHelpers.capitalize-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"capitalize(x)\n\nUppercase the first character of x. Use directly as a filter: {{ name |> capitalize }}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers.capitalize"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Ginger.DefaultHelpers.default_to-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"default_to(fallback)\n\nReturn a filter that substitutes fallback when the piped value is an Undefined context variable (with undefined = :lenient or :default).\n\n{{ nickname |> default_to(\"Anonymous\") }}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers.default_to"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Ginger.DefaultHelpers.excerpt-Tuple{Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"excerpt(n)\n\nReturn a filter that truncates to at most n characters and appends … when the text is longer. n counts characters, not bytes.\n\n{{ post.body |> excerpt(80) }}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers.excerpt"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Ginger.DefaultHelpers.join_with","page":"API reference","text":"join_with(sep = \"\")\n\nReturn a filter that joins the elements of an iterable with sep.\n\n{{ tags |> join_with(\", \") }}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers.join_with"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Ginger.DefaultHelpers.lower-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"lower(x)\n\nConvert x to lowercase text. Use directly as a filter: {{ name |> lower }}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers.lower"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Ginger.DefaultHelpers.replace_with","page":"API reference","text":"replace_with(from, to = \"\")\n\nReturn a filter that replaces each occurrence of from with to. from and to are passed to Base.replace, so they can be strings, characters, or regexes.\n\n{{ handle |> replace_with(\"@example.com\", \"\") }}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers.replace_with"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Ginger.DefaultHelpers.starts_with-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"starts_with(prefix)\n\nReturn Base.Fix2(startswith, prefix), a filter that tests whether the piped value starts with prefix.\n\n{{ path |> starts_with(\"/admin\") }}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers.starts_with"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Ginger.DefaultHelpers.title-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"title(x)\n\nTitle-case x. Use directly as a filter: {{ heading |> title }}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers.title"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Ginger.DefaultHelpers.trim-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"trim(x)\n\nRemove leading and trailing whitespace from x. Use directly as a filter: {{ value |> trim }}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers.trim"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Ginger.DefaultHelpers.truncate_at-Tuple{Integer}","page":"API reference","text":"truncate_at(n)\n\nReturn Base.Fix2(first, n), a filter that keeps the first n elements of an iterable or the first n characters of a string.\n\n{{ title |> truncate_at(20) }}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers.truncate_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Ginger.DefaultHelpers.upper-Tuple{Any}","page":"API reference","text":"upper(x)\n\nConvert x to uppercase text. Use directly as a filter: {{ name |> upper }}. The result is a plain String, so autoescaping still applies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Ginger.DefaultHelpers.upper"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance/#Composition-and-inheritance","page":"Composition and inheritance","text":"Ginger resolves all template references—extends, include, import, from—inside the enclosing @template / @templates unit and compiles them into the same expansion. Ginger compiles a template that several places reference only once, and a reference cycle is a compile-time error.","title":"Composition and inheritance"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance/#Macros","page":"Composition and inheritance","text":"A macro is a reusable fragment that compiles to a generated function. The function builds an IOBuffer and returns an HTMLString, so a macro result is not escaped twice:\n\n{% macro badge(text, kind = \"info\") %}<span class=\"{{ kind }}\">{{ text }}</span>{% endmacro %}\n{{ badge(\"Hi\") }}\n\nA macro body sees its arguments, the template's other macros, and host-module helpers. It does not see the caller's context, and a free variable that is not one of those is a compile-time error. Macro definitions must appear at the top level, and Ginger rejects duplicate macro names.","title":"Macros"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance/#Includes","page":"Composition and inheritance","text":"{% include %} renders another template in place. The render context passes through, and with adds or overrides bindings:\n\n{% include \"partials/head.html\" %}\n{% include \"partials/head.html\" with heading = \"Posts\" %}\n\nOnly the render context crosses an include boundary. A loop variable or other local is not visible inside the included template unless the caller passes it with with. The included template gets a fresh block namespace, so its own blocks resolve to its own defaults.","title":"Includes"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance/#Imports","page":"Composition and inheritance","text":"{% import %} binds another template's macro namespace, and {% from %} binds individual macros:\n\n{% import \"forms.html\" as forms %}\n{{ forms.field(\"email\") }}\n\n{% from \"forms.html\" import field, label as lbl %}\n{{ lbl(\"Email\") }} {{ field(\"email\") }}","title":"Imports"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance/#Inheritance","page":"Composition and inheritance","text":"{% extends %} composes a child template with a base. The child overrides named {% block %} regions, and output outside a block is a compile-time error:\n\n{# base.html #}\n<html><body>{% block content %}nothing yet{% endblock %}</body></html>\n\n{# index.html #}\n{% extends \"base.html\" %}\n{% block content %}<h1>{{ user }}</h1>{% endblock %}\n\nA block that a child does not override falls back to the base's default. super() renders the nearest ancestor's version of the block, and super(n) the version n levels up:\n\n{% extends \"base.html\" %}\n{% block content %}<main>{{ super() }}</main>{% endblock %}\n\nNested blocks and multi-level inheritance work, and a child's macros and imports are visible inside its blocks.","title":"Inheritance"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance/#How-it-compiles","page":"Composition and inheritance","text":"Composition is entirely static. Each template compiles to a body function and one function per block. A child body passes its blocks to its parent with merge:\n\n@noinline function __ginger_body_index__(out, ctx, blocks)\n    return __ginger_body_base__(out, ctx, merge(__ginger_blocks_index__, blocks))\nend\n\nIncoming blocks from a more-derived template win, which is why the child's own blocks are the first argument to merge. A block site dispatches through the resulting concrete NamedTuple, which constant-folds. super() is a direct call on a compile-time-known function, so there is no runtime block registry and no super object.","title":"How it compiles"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance/#Restrictions","page":"Composition and inheritance","text":"Ginger checks these restrictions at macro-expansion time:\n\n{% extends %} must appear once, at the top level.\n{% block %} may not appear under control flow. Nested blocks directly inside a block are allowed.\n{% macro %} must appear at the top level.\nBlock and macro names must be unique within a template.\nAn extending template may not emit non-whitespace output outside a block.\nsuper() may only appear inside a block.","title":"Restrictions"},{"category":"section","location":"inheritance/#Cross-package-extends","page":"Composition and inheritance","text":"All template references resolve within one @templates unit. Ginger does not support cross-package extends. If several packages need to share a base template, keep the base in one unit and re-export the compiled Template, or copy the base into the consuming package's unit.","title":"Cross-package extends"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-from-otera/#Migrating-from-OteraEngine","page":"Migrating from OteraEngine","text":"Ginger shares OteraEngine's Jinja-like surface ({{ }}, {% %}, {# #}, |> filters, {% extends %} / {% block %}), but the execution model is different. This page lists the differences and a migration path.","title":"Migrating from OteraEngine"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-from-otera/#The-conceptual-shift","page":"Migrating from OteraEngine","text":"OteraEngine builds a renderer when you call Template(path) at runtime. Ginger compiles a template during macro expansion, when the host package is parsed:\n\n# OteraEngine\ntmp = Template(\"templates/index.html\")\ntmp(init = Dict(:user => \"frank\"))\n\n# Ginger\n@template \"templates/index.html\" as INDEX\nrender(INDEX; user = \"frank\")\n\nGinger has no runtime Template(...) constructor. The compiled value is a Template that holds the generated entry function, and render calls it. Because compilation is part of parsing the host package, the precompile image caches the result and no template is parsed at runtime.\n\nTopic Ginger OteraEngine\nWhen compiled macro expansion Template(...) call\nContext inferred from free variables inferred from undefined symbols\nImplementation synthetic Julia source + Julia parser template AST\nFilters |> with user-curried arguments |> with a zero-argument filter name\nScope Julia scoping Julia-ish\nextends static, same unit literal path\nBlocks static function composition text substitution\nsuper.super() super(2) super.super()\nConfig immutable Config TOML / Dict\nSafe value HTMLString SafeString","title":"The conceptual shift"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-from-otera/#Step-by-step","page":"Migrating from OteraEngine","text":"","title":"Step by step"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-from-otera/#1.-Compile-templates-at-package-scope","page":"Migrating from OteraEngine","text":"Replace runtime construction with @template or @templates. @templates discovers a whole directory and binds a NamedTuple:\n\nmodule MyApp\n\nusing Ginger\nusing Ginger.DefaultHelpers\n\n@templates \"templates\" as TPL\n\nend","title":"1. Compile templates at package scope"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-from-otera/#2.-Render-with-keywords,-not-init","page":"Migrating from OteraEngine","text":"OteraEngine passes a Dict:\n\ntmp(init = Dict(:user => \"frank\", :posts => posts))\n\nGinger passes keyword arguments, and the generated function specializes on the context NamedTuple:\n\nrender(TPL.index; user = \"frank\", posts = posts)","title":"2. Render with keywords, not init"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-from-otera/#3.-Rename-safe-values","page":"Migrating from OteraEngine","text":"OteraEngine.SafeString and safe become HTMLString and safe. Both are idempotent, so {{ x |> safe }} keeps working.","title":"3. Rename safe values"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-from-otera/#4.-Replace-@filter-registrations","page":"Migrating from OteraEngine","text":"OteraEngine registers filters with @filter and looks them up by name at render time. Ginger has no registry. A filter is any function in the host module, and a parameterized filter returns a callable.\n\n# OteraEngine\n@filter repeat say_twice(txt) = txt * txt\n\n# Ginger: a one-argument function is used directly\nsay_twice(txt) = txt * txt\n\nA filter that needs an argument returns a callable:\n\nexcerpt(n) = s -> length(s) <= n ? s : first(s, n) * \"…\"\n\n{{ body |> excerpt(80) }}\n\nOteraEngine's built-in upper, lower, escape, e, and safe have Ginger equivalents in DefaultHelpers. Import the module with using Ginger.DefaultHelpers. quote_sql has no equivalent. Write it as a host function if you need it.","title":"4. Replace @filter registrations"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-from-otera/#5.-Port-control-blocks","page":"Migrating from OteraEngine","text":"if, for, and let map directly. Ginger also accepts {% endfor %}, {% endif %}, {% endwhile %}, and {% endlet %} as aliases for {% end %}. OteraEngine's {% set %} becomes an ordinary Julia assignment:\n\n{% x = compute() %}\n\nOteraEngine's {% end %} is accepted unchanged.","title":"5. Port control blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-from-otera/#6.-Port-inheritance","page":"Migrating from OteraEngine","text":"{% extends %}, {% block %}, {% endblock %}, and {% include %} map directly, and all references resolve inside the @templates unit. super() maps directly, and super.super() becomes super(2).\n\nGinger does not support cross-package extends. Keep shared bases inside the same unit.","title":"6. Port inheritance"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-from-otera/#7.-Port-macros","page":"Migrating from OteraEngine","text":"{% macro name(args) %}…{% endmacro %} maps directly. Ginger macros compile to functions that return HTMLString, and Ginger collects them in the template's namespace for {% import %} and {% from %}. Ginger rejects duplicate names, and a macro body sees its arguments and host helpers, not the caller's context.","title":"7. Port macros"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-from-otera/#8.-Port-configuration","page":"Migrating from OteraEngine","text":"OteraEngine reads a TOML file or a Dict. Ginger takes an immutable Config value as a macro argument:\n\n@template \"page.html\" as PAGE config = Config(\n    autoescape = false,\n    trim_blocks = true,\n    lstrip_blocks = true,\n    undefined = :lenient,\n)","title":"8. Port configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-from-otera/#Gotchas","page":"Migrating from OteraEngine","text":"Whitespace. OteraEngine defaults to autospace = true. Ginger defaults to trim_blocks = false and lstrip_blocks = false. Set autospace = true to match. Ginger also trims the newline after expression tags when trim_blocks is on, so keep the newline with + if that matters.\nContext collisions. A name that is a host-module global (a helper, a Base export) is not a context variable. Do not use a variable name that is also a filter name. Rename the render keyword instead.\nNo runtime paths. A template path is a string literal known at macro expansion. Dynamic template selection needs an if/elseif over statically compiled templates.\nPrecompilation. Editing a template invalidates the host package and triggers a reload. Use Revise.jl or reload the package.","title":"Gotchas"},{"category":"section","location":"syntax/#Syntax","page":"Syntax","text":"","title":"Syntax"},{"category":"section","location":"syntax/#Delimiters","page":"Syntax","text":"Form Meaning\n{{ expr }} Julia expression, HTML-escaped by default\n{% stmt %} Julia statements and structural tags\n{# … #} Comment. Nestable, emits nothing\n{% raw %} … {% endraw %} Literal text. Ginger never interprets delimiters inside\n\nGinger configures all three delimiter pairs through Config.","title":"Delimiters"},{"category":"section","location":"syntax/#Whitespace-control","page":"Syntax","text":"A - on the inside edge of a tag strips adjacent whitespace. A + forces it to be preserved:\n\n{{- value -}}      {# strip both sides #}\n{%- if x +%}…{% endif %}\n{#- comment -#}\n\nTwo config flags handle the common cases:\n\ntrim_blocks removes the first newline after a block tag.\nlstrip_blocks removes whitespace from the start of a line up to a block tag.\nautospace = true enables both. autospace = false disables both.\n\nGinger applies trim_blocks after expression tags as well as block tags. Jinja applies it to block tags only. If exact parity with Jinja matters, keep the trailing newline with + or turn trim_blocks off.\n\nlstrip_blocks never eats significant indentation after text, and the trim_blocks/lstrip_blocks flags never touch {% raw %} content.","title":"Whitespace control"},{"category":"section","location":"syntax/#Comments","page":"Syntax","text":"{# a comment; {# comments nest #} #}","title":"Comments"},{"category":"section","location":"syntax/#Statements-and-control-flow","page":"Syntax","text":"{% %} contains arbitrary Julia statements. Ginger emits the leading keyword verbatim, so Julia's parser matches the structure:\n\n{% if user.admin %}\n  <a href=\"/admin\">Admin</a>\n{% elseif user.member %}\n  Member\n{% else %}\n  Guest\n{% endif %}\n\nif / elseif / else, for, while, let, begin, try / catch / finally, function, do blocks, and quote all work. Close them with {% end %} or the alias endif, endfor, endwhile, endlet, endblock, endmacro. Ginger does not verify alias mismatch. Julia reports the resulting structure error at the template offset.\n\nAssignments and other statements work too:\n\n{% x = compute(a, b) %}\n{% function local_helper(v) %}\n  return v * 2\n{% end %}","title":"Statements and control flow"},{"category":"section","location":"syntax/#for-…-else","page":"Syntax","text":"Julia has no for/else, so Ginger lowers it. The else body runs only when the iterator produces nothing:\n\n{% for post in posts %}\n  <article>{{ post.title }}</article>\n{% else %}\n  <p>No posts yet.</p>\n{% endfor %}","title":"for … else"},{"category":"section","location":"syntax/#Expressions","page":"Syntax","text":"{{ }} contains an arbitrary Julia expression:\n\n{{ user.name }}\n{{ posts[1].title }}\n{{ length(posts) == 0 ? \"none\" : \"some\" }}\n\nGinger HTML-escapes every expression by default. See Filters and helpers for the escaping rules and the safe bypass.","title":"Expressions"},{"category":"section","location":"syntax/#Raw","page":"Syntax","text":"{% raw %}\n  {{ this is literal }}\n  {% if this is literal too %}\n{% endraw %}\n\nExplicit - markers on the two raw tags trim the body edges. trim_blocks and lstrip_blocks never modify raw text.","title":"Raw"},{"category":"section","location":"precompilation/#Templates-and-precompilation","page":"Templates and precompilation","text":"","title":"Templates and precompilation"},{"category":"section","location":"precompilation/#@template-and-@templates","page":"Templates and precompilation","text":"@template compiles one file and binds a Template const:\n\n@template \"views/index.html\" as INDEX\nrender(INDEX; user = \"frank\")\n\nThe default const name is the uppercased file stem. Ginger resolves a relative path against the directory of the file that contains the macro call.\n\n@templates discovers every file under a directory recursively and binds a NamedTuple of Templates named TEMPLATES (override with as NAME). Subdirectories become nested NamedTuples and a file is keyed by its stem, so views/partials/head.html is TPL.partials.head:\n\nmodule MyApp\n\nusing Ginger\n\n@templates \"views\" as TPL\n\nend\n\nrender(TPL.index; user = \"frank\")          # views/index.html\nrender(TPL.partials.head)                  # views/partials/head.html\n\nGinger discovers a file whatever its extension, and skips hidden entries (a leading .). Two files whose stems map to the same key, or a file that collides with a subdirectory name, is a compile-time ArgumentError.","title":"@template and @templates"},{"category":"section","location":"precompilation/#Helpers","page":"Templates and precompilation","text":"helpers = (MyHelpers, MyFilters) emits using MyHelpers, MyFilters into the host module before Ginger compiles the templates, so the exported functions and macros of those modules are available in every template:\n\n@templates \"views\" helpers = (MyFilters,)\n\nThe entries must name modules, not values.","title":"Helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"precompilation/#Precompilation","page":"Templates and precompilation","text":"@templates and @template read every template and every directory at macro-expansion time and register them with Base.include_dependency. The generated functions therefore land in the host package's precompile image, and no template is parsed or compiled at runtime.\n\nThere is no Module, no Core.eval, no cache, no lock, no dev-mode hashing, and no world-age handling. Config is an immutable value passed as a macro argument.","title":"Precompilation"},{"category":"section","location":"precompilation/#The-dev-loop","page":"Templates and precompilation","text":"The dependency registration drives development. Editing a template changes the package's precompile key, so the next using MyApp recompiles it. Adding a template changes the recorded directory contents, so a new file is discovered and bound on the next load. Removing a template invalidates the package as well.\n\nGinger does not implement its own file watching or hashing. It delegates that work to the standard Julia dev loop. Reload the package, or use Revise.jl, and the macros re-expand.\n\nBecause Ginger compiles the whole set in one expansion, a template that is only used internally (a partial, a macro library) still becomes a key in the NamedTuple. Use the keys you need and ignore the rest.","title":"The dev loop"},{"category":"section","location":"precompilation/#Single-file","page":"Templates and precompilation","text":"For one file, use @template:\n\n@template \"views/index.html\" as INDEX\nrender(INDEX; user = \"frank\")\n\n@template reads the file at expansion time and registers it with include_dependency in the same way.","title":"Single file"},{"category":"section","location":"precompilation/#Inline-templates","page":"Templates and precompilation","text":"For a template that lives in the source, use the ginger\"…\" string macro. It compiles during expansion and returns a Template:\n\nconst GREETING = ginger\"Hello {{ name }}!\"\nrender(GREETING; name = \"frank\")     # -> \"Hello frank!\"\nGREETING(name = \"frank\")             # the same\n\nAny reference inside the string resolves relative to the file that contains the macro call. Because the source is part of the host package's AST, an inline template is precompiled like a file-based one and needs no include_dependency entry.","title":"Inline templates"},{"category":"section","location":"precompilation/#Cost-model","page":"Templates and precompilation","text":"No runtime template discovery or dynamic paths.\nName isolation comes from mangled identifiers rather than modules.\nAdding a template file requires recompiling the host package.\nCross-package {% extends %} is limited (see Composition and inheritance).","title":"Cost model"},{"category":"section","location":"context/#Context","page":"Context","text":"Templates do not declare their context. Ginger infers it at macro-expansion time from the free variables of the generated body.\n\nPass the context as keyword arguments:\n\nrender(TPL.index; user = \"frank\", posts = posts)","title":"Context"},{"category":"section","location":"context/#What-counts-as-context","page":"Context","text":"A name in a template is one of three things:\n\nA local. Loop binders, let bindings, function and do parameters, assignments, and this template's own blocks and macros are not context.\nA host-module global. Ginger treats a name that is defined or imported in the host module, or exported by a module passed to helpers, as a global. This is how helper functions and filters work without a registry.\nA context variable. Ginger binds everything else from the render context.\n\nGinger classifies names with a free-variable analysis over the parsed template body. The generated prologue reads each context variable from the context NamedTuple.","title":"What counts as context"},{"category":"section","location":"context/#Undefined-variables","page":"Context","text":"A missing context variable follows the undefined config mode:\n\nMode Behaviour\n:strict (default) Throw MissingContextVariable\n:lenient Bind an Undefined singleton. It renders as \"\" and throws on property or index access.\n:default Bind Undefined. The default(value, fallback) helper substitutes a fallback.\n\n@template \"page.html\" as PAGE config = Config(undefined = :lenient)\n\n{{ nickname |> default_to(\"Anonymous\") }}","title":"Undefined variables"},{"category":"section","location":"context/#Caveat:-names-that-collide-with-host-globals","page":"Context","text":"Ginger treats a name that is already defined in the host module as a host global, not as a context variable. This behaviour is deliberate. It is how helper functions work without a registry. As a result, a context variable that is named after a Base export or a DefaultHelpers name (for example count, name, title, or missing) resolves to the global instead.\n\nGive such values a different render keyword:\n\nrender(TPL.page; heading = \"Posts\")     # not `title`, which is a helper\n\nThe same rule applies to filters. Do not use a variable name that is also a filter name.","title":"Caveat: names that collide with host globals"},{"category":"section","location":"filters/#Filters-and-helpers","page":"Filters and helpers","text":"","title":"Filters and helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"filters/#Pipe-semantics","page":"Filters and helpers","text":"Filters use Julia's pipe operator with ordinary Julia semantics. The engine does no rewriting:\n\nSource Julia meaning\na |> f f(a)\na |> f(1, 2) f(1, 2)(a)\na |> f(1) |> g g(f(1)(a))\n\nThe rule is: a parameterized filter returns a callable.\n\nupper(s)       = uppercase(s)\nexcerpt(n)     = s -> length(s) <= n ? s : first(s, n) * \"…\"\ntruncate_at(n) = Base.Fix2(first, n)\n\nGinger does not reinterpret a bare |. It remains Julia's bitor.","title":"Pipe semantics"},{"category":"section","location":"filters/#Autoescaping","page":"Filters and helpers","text":"Ginger emits every {{ }} as escape(expr). escape is idempotent on HTMLString, so {{ x |> safe }} and {{ safe(x) }} pass through unchanged. Ginger escapes {{ x |> escape }} once, not twice.\n\nWhen the outermost expression is statically known to produce an HTMLString—a call to safe, escape, HTMLString, super(), or a template macro—Ginger elides the redundant escape wrapper at compile time. The output is identical.\n\nSet autoescape = false to disable escaping:\n\n@template \"email.txt\" as EMAIL config = Config(autoescape = false)","title":"Autoescaping"},{"category":"section","location":"filters/#DefaultHelpers","page":"Filters and helpers","text":"Import the module into the host module to make its names resolvable from templates:\n\nusing Ginger\nusing Ginger.DefaultHelpers\n\nHelper Form\nescape, safe one argument, idempotent\nupper, lower, title, capitalize, trim one argument\nexcerpt(n), truncate_at(n) parameterized, returns a callable\nreplace_with(from, to = \"\") parameterized\njoin_with(sep = \"\") parameterized\nstarts_with(prefix) parameterized\ndefault_to(fallback) parameterized, for undefined = :lenient\n\n{{ user.name |> upper }}\n{{ post.body |> excerpt(80) |> safe }}\n{{ heading |> trim |> capitalize }}\n{{ path |> starts_with(\"/admin\") }}\n\nGinger re-exports escape, safe, HTMLString, and default. Generated code references escape fully qualified, so autoescaping works even without the import.","title":"DefaultHelpers"},{"category":"section","location":"filters/#Custom-helpers","page":"Filters and helpers","text":"Any function that the host module defines or imports is callable from a template, with no registry and no declaration:\n\nmodule MyApp\n\nusing Ginger\nusing Ginger.DefaultHelpers\ninclude(\"filters.jl\")           # slugify, excerpt, …\n\n@template \"templates/page.html\" as PAGE\n\nend\n\nAlternatively, @templates accepts helpers = (MyFilters,) and emits using MyFilters into the host module:\n\n@templates \"views\" helpers = (MyFilters,)\n\nThe entries must name modules, not values.","title":"Custom helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"errors/#Errors","page":"Errors","text":"Ginger reports two kinds of problems: compile-time template errors and render-time provenance.","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"errors/#Compile-time-diagnostics","page":"Errors","text":"A template that Ginger cannot lex, parse, or normalize raises TemplateSyntaxError. When the offending template text is available, the error renders the line with a caret:\n\nTemplateSyntaxError: unexpected `)`\n  --> templates/syntax_err.html:3:10\n    |\n  3 | {{ 1 + }}\n    |          ^\n\nThe line and caret come from the synthetic-to-template offset map, which records one position per emitted chunk. The diagnostic points at the template line where the offending chunk starts, with the caret column clamped to that line.\n\nGinger's own checks—restrictions, duplicate names, unresolved references, and reserved-prefix misuse—carry a Pos directly and render the same way.","title":"Compile-time diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"errors/#Runtime-positions","page":"Errors","text":"Every statement carries a LineNumberNode with the virtual template path and the real template line, so an uncaught exception backtraces to templates/index.html:3. The parser receives the virtual path as its filename, so @__FILE__ expands to the virtual path, @__LINE__ to the template line, and @__DIR__ to the virtual directory. No source rewriting is necessary.\n\nVirtual paths are package-relative, never absolute build-time paths, so precompile images stay relocatable. Config.source_root resolves them to absolute paths for display and editor jumps.","title":"Runtime positions"},{"category":"section","location":"errors/#Provenance-without-a-frame-stack","page":"Errors","text":"Ginger marks __ginger_body_*, __ginger_block_*, and __ginger_macro_* functions as @noinline and registers them in a compile-time provenance registry. render catches an exception, walks the native backtrace with Base.StackTraces, and builds the chain:\n\nTemplateError: MissingContextVariable: context variable `user` was not passed\n  in block \"content\" at templates/index.html:3\n  at templates/base.html:12\n  rendered from app.jl:20\n\nAn exception with no template frame propagates unchanged, so a helper called outside a template keeps its own exception type.","title":"Provenance without a frame stack"},{"category":"section","location":"errors/#template_backtrace","page":"Errors","text":"template_backtrace(err) returns the chain as a Vector{TemplateFrame}, innermost first. Each frame carries:\n\nkind: :body, :block, :macro, or :render,\nname: the block or macro name when one applies,\npath: the package-relative virtual path,\nabs_path: the path resolved against Config.source_root,\nline and col.\n\nThe no-argument form returns the chain of the TemplateError currently being handled, or an empty vector when there is none.","title":"template_backtrace"}]
}
